stories.agent_instructions:
WHAT: a branch that changes production paths outside its story's `files` is sent back for rework at `pipeline/review_orchestrator.py` (`_scope_violations` / the `if _scope_violations:` block). When the plan simply failed to declare the path, that rework can never succeed: `pipeline/triage_scope_actions.py` `check_scope_amendment` refuses any path whose directory is not ALREADY declared (`outside_declared_packages`), plus high-risk stories entirely, so no agent action can widen the scope. Live on plan `standalone-server` (2026-10-30): five stories parked on scope-gate findings and burned 3-5 rework cycles each, and SA-00 was unwinnable - the gate ordered the agent to revert `docs/specs/IDENTITY_AND_POLICY.md` while the reviewer blocked because that canonical spec was stale. The park reason users see is "triage attempts at cap", which reads as an agent failure and sends them looking at the wrong thing.

CHANGE 1 - new module `pipeline/scope_gate_park.py`:
- A frozen dataclass `Park` with one field, `reason: str`.
- `violation_paths(violations: list[str]) -> list[str]`: `line.split(": ", 1)[0]` for each line. `scope_violations` in `pipeline/scope_gate.py` emits exactly two shapes today: "<path>: outside this story's `files` scope" and "<top>/: new top-level package not present at the branch base". A line with no ": " keeps the whole line as the path; the amendment guardrail then refuses it as malformed, which parks - fail closed, never guess.
- `decide(story: dict, violations: list[str]) -> Park | None`: parse the paths, call `check_scope_amendment(story, paths)` (import that function; it is a pure guardrail and importing it creates no cycle). Return a `Park` whose reason is `scope gate: <comma-joined paths> outside the plan's `files`; needs a plan edit` when ANY of these holds: `story.get("scope_conflict")` is present (the agent already reported the conflict), the guardrail refuses EVERY path (`not allowed`), or `story.get("scope_gate_hits", 0) >= 1`. Otherwise increment `story["scope_gate_hits"]` and return `None`, so the ordinary one-time send-back still happens.
- `feedback(violations: list[str]) -> str`: return exactly today's text - `SCOPE_GATE_HEADER + "\n" + "\n".join(f"- Blocking: {v}" for v in violations) + "\n" + SCOPE_GATE_REMEDY + "\n" + "VERDICT: REQUEST_CHANGES"`.
- `run(story, worktree, base, check, notify, plan_name, story_key, cid_kwargs)`: call `check(worktree, base, story["files"])`, then `decide`, then `notify(plan_name, message, event="scope_gate_failed", story_key=story_key, **cid_kwargs)` with a message that says how many paths and whether it parked ("needs a plan edit") or is being sent back, and return a small result carrying `.feedback` (the text, or None when there were no violations) and `.park`.
- This module mutates `story` (the counter, and the status/reason when it parks) and performs no git or file I/O of its own. Do not import `pipeline.review_orchestrator`.

CHANGE 2 - `pipeline/review_orchestrator.py`, which is at EXACTLY 1000 lines: your net change to it must be NEGATIVE. Replace the 4-line `_scope_violations = (...)` assignment AND the whole 13-line `if _scope_violations:` block with a `run(...)` call plus:
  if _gate.park is not None:
      story["status"] = "parked"; story["parked_reason"] = _gate.park.reason
      commit_story(plan_name, story_key, story, manifest_path)
      return {"ok": True, "verdict": "REQUEST_CHANGES", "status": "parked",
              "parked_reason": _gate.park.reason, "pr_url": story.get("pr_url")}
  if _gate.feedback is not None:
      reviewer_output = _gate.feedback
  elif skip_llm_reviewer:
      ...unchanged...
`commit_story` is already imported in that module. Mirror the return shape the function already uses at its end. Keep the review gate's existing behaviour and text intact: it still runs before any LLM review, still suppresses the review on a violation, still sends the branch back once with character-identical feedback, still never gates a story that declares no `files`, and leaves a clean branch untouched.

TESTS (new file tests/unit/test_scope_gate_park.py), each a separate test:
- `violation_paths` parses both shapes (one for "<path>: outside ...", one for "<top>/: new top-level package ...") and keeps a line with no ": " intact;
- first violation, amendable paths, no recorded conflict -> `decide` returns None and sets `scope_gate_hits` to 1;
- second violation with `scope_gate_hits` already 1 -> Park whose reason names the path and the plan edit;
- `story["scope_conflict"]` present -> Park on the very first call;
- every path refused by the guardrail (e.g. declared dirs are {"pipeline"} and the path is "app/x.py") -> Park on the first call;
- high-risk story -> Park on the first call (the guardrail refuses high-risk outright);
- `feedback` reproduces the exact header, remedy and verdict lines;
- INTEGRATION (grades the wiring, not just the unit): call the real `review_story` with `pipeline.review_orchestrator.check_branch_scope` monkeypatched to return one violation, a story whose `scope_gate_hits` is already 1 and whose `files` are declared, and assert the returned dict has `status == "parked"` and a `parked_reason` naming the plan edit. Follow the harness of an existing test that drives `review_story` end to end (search tests/unit for ones calling `review_story`) rather than inventing one.

Engineering rules: write the failing test first and confirm it fails on the assertion (not an import error), then implement. Run the full suite (`.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q`) and `.venv/bin/python -m ruff check .` before finishing. Keep every touched .py file under 1000 lines - `review_orchestrator.py` must end shorter than it starts. Do not modify any existing test unless this brief pre-authorizes the exact edit; there are no such edits here, so if an existing test pins the block you are moving, stop and report it via `SCOPE-CONFLICT: <path> :: <why>` instead of editing it.

WHAT: `pipeline/ingest.py` already warns at ingest when a declared `files` entry has no line-limit headroom (`scope_headroom_warning`, `pipeline/scope_headroom.py`). It has no check for the opposite and more common authoring error: the brief names a production path the story's `files` omits. That omission makes the story unwinnable, because the scope gate sends the branch back and no agent action can widen scope (see `check_scope_amendment` in `pipeline/triage_scope_actions.py`). Live on `standalone-server` 2026-10-30: SA-01's brief discussed `pipeline/service.py` while the change also had to gate `app/dashboard_routes.py`, and SA-08's brief named two deploy files while the change needed four.

CHANGE 1 - `pipeline/scope_headroom.py`: widen the module into the ingest-time scope-advisory module (update its module docstring to say so; it now answers two authoring questions, not one). Add `brief_path_warning(story: dict, repo_root: str) -> str | None`:
- Return None unless `story["files"]` is a list and `agent_instructions` is a str.
- Extract candidate paths from `agent_instructions` with a single regex for a token that contains a `/` and ends in one of `.py`, `.md`, `.sh`, `.yaml`, `.yml`, `.json`, `.toml`, `.plist` - e.g. `[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]*\.(?:py|md|sh|ya?ml|json|toml|plist)`. Strip trailing punctuation (`.`, `,`, `)`, `:`, `;`) from each match.
- Ignore a match that is already declared, that `pipeline.scope_gate.is_test_path` calls a test path, or that starts with `http`. Ignore paths under `docs/plans/` (plan documents are not story scope).
- Return a message naming up to five undeclared paths and the advice `declare them in the story's files scope, or say in the brief that they are deliberately out of scope`; None when there are none. Advisory only: catch every exception and return None, exactly as `scope_headroom_warning` does.
- Add `scope_advisories(story: dict, repo_root: str)` yielding `(message, event_name)` pairs: `(scope_headroom_warning(...), "scope_headroom")` and `(brief_path_warning(...), "scope_brief_paths")`, skipping the None ones.

CHANGE 2 - `pipeline/ingest.py` (998 lines, so net +1 line at most): replace the existing three lines
    headroom = scope_headroom_warning(story, repo_root)
    if headroom:
        _notify_user(plan_name, f"{key}: {headroom}", event="scope_headroom", story_key=key)
        logging.getLogger("pipeline").warning(f"{plan_name}/{key}: {headroom}")
with a loop over `scope_advisories(story, repo_root)` that does the same two calls per pair, using the pair's event name (`event=event_name`). No other change in that file; drop the now-unused `scope_headroom_warning` import only if the loop imports `scope_advisories` instead.

TESTS (new file tests/unit/test_brief_scope_warning.py), each a separate test:
- a brief naming `app/dashboard_routes.py` with `files` of ["pipeline/service.py"] -> warning naming it;
- a brief naming a declared path -> None;
- a brief naming only a test path (`tests/unit/test_x.py`) -> None;
- a brief naming a path under `docs/plans/` -> None;
- an empty or missing brief -> None, and a story with no `files` -> None;
- a URL (`https://github.com/x/y/blob/z.py`) -> None;
- more than five undeclared paths -> the message names five and does not raise;
- `scope_advisories` yields the headroom pair and the brief-path pair when both apply, and only the non-None ones otherwise (stub the two warning functions with monkeypatch).

Engineering rules: write the failing test first and confirm it fails on the assertion (not an import error), then implement. Run the full suite (`.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q`) and `.venv/bin/python -m ruff check .` before finishing. Keep every touched .py file under 1000 lines. Do not modify any existing test; if one pins `scope_headroom_warning`'s signature or `ingest.py`'s notification text, stop and report it via `SCOPE-CONFLICT: <path> :: <why>` instead of editing it.

WHAT: `deploy/entrypoint.sh` (story SA-08 of plan `standalone-server`) exports the operator's Anthropic key as `PIPELINE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for the `claude` CLI. `app/backend_claude.py` (story SA-09) converts it to `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for the `claude` subprocess and pops the carrier, but the raw name still sits in the server process environment, so every dispatched agent spawned through `pipeline/execution.py` inherits it. `_AGENT_SECRET_ENV` is the frozenset of variable names stripped from an agent's child environment; `PIPELINE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is not in it. SA-09 identified this, correctly found it outside its `files` scope, reverted it and deferred it to this story.

CHANGE - `pipeline/execution.py`: add `"PIPELINE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"` to the `_AGENT_SECRET_ENV` frozenset, with a comment saying why: the claude driver converts it to ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and drops the carrier, but a prompt-injected agent must never see the raw mounted secret name either. Place the entry beside the other provider credentials, before the closing brace. Do not change `_strip_agent_secrets`, `_local_agent_env` or any other function - they are already correct and this is the only missing entry.

TESTS (new file tests/unit/test_agent_secret_env.py), each a separate test:
- `"PIPELINE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" in pipeline.execution._AGENT_SECRET_ENV` (membership, never an exact-set or count assertion: the frozenset is cumulative and later stories add entries);
- `_local_agent_env({"PIPELINE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY": "sk-x", "PATH": "/bin"})` returns `{"PATH": "/bin"}` - grades the wiring that actually strips the env, not just the constant;
- `_local_agent_env` with `None` materialises `os.environ` and still omits `PIPELINE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` when that variable is set in the test's monkeypatched environment;
- `_strip_agent_secrets` keeps a non-secret variable such as `PIPELINE_MODEL_REGISTRY_PATH`.

Engineering rules: write the failing test first and confirm it fails on the assertion (not an import error), then implement. Run the full suite (`.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q`) and `.venv/bin/python -m ruff check .` before finishing. Keep every touched .py file under 1000 lines. Never log or echo a secret. Do not modify any existing test; if one pins `_AGENT_SECRET_ENV` by exact equality or count it will fail - that is the pre-authorized-conflict case, so stop and report it via `SCOPE-CONFLICT: <path> :: <why>` rather than editing it.
